[Line "English Attack: 6...e6, the Scheveningen set-up with ...b5 and ...Bb7"]
[Section "6.Be3: ...e6 and ...Ng4"]
[Summary "Black keeps d5 under control and plays ...b5, ...Bb7 and ...h6, then breaks with ...e5 and ...d5. It is a second choice: the line below is given by the study without commentary, and 7.g4 leads to the Perenyi Attack."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e6 { [%csl Yd5,Yf5] The Scheveningen move order.
The pawn guards d5 and f5, so White's knights have no outposts, but the bishop on c8 is blocked and the pawn
no longer kicks the knight from d4: White gets a free hand with the pawn storm. Black usually has to spend
a tempo on ...e5 later. } 7. f3 { [%cal Gg2g4,Gd1d2] } ( 7. g4 { The Perenyi Attack. } 7... e5 8. Nf5 g6 9. g5
gxf5 10. gxf6 Qxf6 { Everything is forced: the knights are exchanged, and after 11.exf5 both sides have
shattered kingside pawns, the g-file is open and the kings are exposed. The studies I used stop at 9.g5, so
this line should be checked before it is played. } ) 7... b5 { [%cal Gb5b4,Gc8b7] } 8. Qd2 Bb7 { The bishop
takes the long diagonal at once; it will also support ...d5 later. } 9. g4 h6 { [%cal Gh6g5] Takes g5 from the
pawn, so that the knight on f6 is not driven away. } 10. h4 Nbd7 11. Rg1 e5 { [%cal Ge5d4] The pawn comes to
e5 at last, with tempo on the knight, and Black's pieces now cover d5. } 12. Nf5 d5 { [%cal Gd5e4] Hits the
pawn on e4 and the knight on f5 is no longer supported by the e-pawn. } 13. Nxd5 Nxd5 14. exd5 g6 { [%cal
Gg6f5] } 15. Ng3 Nf6 { [%cal Gf6d5] **Verdict:** playable and unclear. Black has to win back the pawn on d5
with ...Nf6 and ...Bxd5 and has the better development, but the study gives no further moves and no
verdict, so the line should be treated as a second choice behind 6...e5. } *

[Line "English Attack: 6...Ng4, the Anti-English"]
[Section "6.Be3: ...e6 and ...Ng4"]
[Summary "Black attacks the bishop at once. If White retreats with Bc1 Black can repeat; if he plays Bg5 Black gets a wild kingside pawn structure with ...h6 and ...g5 and a knight on e5."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 Ng4 { [%cal Gg4e3] The Anti-English. It attacks
the bishop at once and makes White decide whether to give up the bishop pair or to repeat moves. The study
I used says that Black can also repeat with ...Nf6 and ...Ng4 if White is happy with a draw. } 7. Bg5 { [%cal
Rg5e7] White keeps the bishop and pins the e7 pawn. } ( 7. Bc1 { If White retreats, Black can repeat the moves with ...Nf6 and ...Ng4, or
play the main move 6...e5 after all. } 7... Nf6 8. Be3 e5 ) 7... h6 { [%cal Gh6g5] Asks the bishop what it wants to
do. } 8. Bh4 g5 { Black expands on the kingside early and takes the bishop's retreat squares away. } 9. Bg3 Bg7 {
[%cal Gg7d4] The bishop on g7 eyes d4 and the long diagonal. } 10. h3 { Kicks the knight away. } (
10. f3? { Hits the knight but loses material. } 10... Ne3! { [%cal Ge3c2,Ge3d1] The knight hits the queen and the bishop at once, and the d4 knight cannot be taken. } 11. Qd2 Bxd4 12. Qxd4?? { Greedy. } 12... Nxc2+
{ Forks the king and the queen. } ) 10... Ne5 { **Verdict:** playable, though not the most common line. Black has
weakened the kingside, so he cannot castle short quickly, but the knight on e5 is superb, the bishop on g7 is
strong and White has no good way to attack the pawns. Care is needed with the king: it will probably go long
or stay in the middle. } *
